    "keywords": "Before upgrading Dependency order Provider upgrade EF Core upgrade Extension upgrade Real-time format upgrade PostgreSQL upgrade Rolling upgrade Rollback Verification checklist  BlueTusk product families are independently versioned. Upgrade the lowestdependency first and keep package versions consistent with the family manifest. Record: current and target BlueTusk versions; .NET and EF Core versions; PostgreSQL major/minor version; installed extension versions; persisted Streams/Sync/Live formats; database migrations to apply; and rollback target. Read the relevant stable release notes and API/format compatibility document.The 1.x line follows semantic versioning; an incompatible public contractchange requires a new major version. The release/dependency order is: Provider Streams Sync Live Control Plane ContinuousGraph EF Core and extension packages must use compatible Provider-family versions.Do not mix an arbitrary set of locally built packages because they share asimilar version string. Review: connection-string option changes; public API baseline changes; type inference and catalogue behavior; pooling/reset behavior; multiplexing or pipeline compatibility; authentication defaults; and NativeAOT/trimming annotations. Run focused compatibility tests against every PostgreSQL major used by theapplication. Upgrade the matching EF Core runtime/design packages together. Before applyingmigrations: Inspect changes to PostgreSQL-specific annotations, generated DDL, extensionrequirements and transaction-suppressed operations. Re-run representative LINQqueries and compiled queries. Property-graph migrations generated by the provider use the fluentCreatePropertyGraph builder. Retain the generated vertex/edge configurationin source control and review aliases, key columns, labels, properties, andsource/destination endpoint columns just like ordinary table migrations. Theserialized metadata overload is compatibility-only and should not be used innew migra"
