    "keywords": "Check the database path Check real-time workers Check the hosting platform Recover a degraded deployment  Use separate startup, liveness, and readiness checks. Liveness answers whetherthe process can continue running. Readiness answers whether that instance canserve its workload with the expected database and downstream dependencies. Use the application’s BlueTusk data source and a restricted role to checkconnectivity. Bound the check with a short timeout and cancellation. For awrite workload, confirm that the database is the intended writable primary.Verify the schema or storage contract separately from network reachability.Use fresh, meaningful status for the affected instance. Look at the last committed checkpoint, WAL lag, retained slot bytes, andsuccessful delivery time. Check spooling, retry pressure, and durable statebefore restarting a worker. A healthy HTTP endpoint does not establish thata Streams or Sync pipeline is keeping up. Use real-time operations for recoverysequences and observability for metrics and alerts. On Kubernetes, verify the assigned node is healthy, the intended databaseand storage are available, and the workload is ready on the expectedrevision. Check readiness and endpoint freshness after a rollout or failover.Inspect failed migration jobs and their database state before retrying.Run migrations as a controlled action outside the application startup loop. Fix the underlying connectivity, permission, or resource problem beforerestarting consumers. Confirm that the retained checkpoint resumes and that afresh query matches the served result. Resume traffic gradually and monitorerrors, connection checkout time, and data freshness."
